cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Echo Audit: a methodology for measuring whether civil society input is reflected in multilateral governance outcomes"
message: "If you use this methodology or code, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Pedro
    family-names: dos Santos Maia
    affiliation: SKEMA Business School
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6933-3274"
version: "2.0.0"
date-released: 2026-09-09
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Echo Audit is a three-phase methodology and R toolkit for measuring whether
  civil society input into a multilateral governance process is reflected in
  that process's agenda and outputs, or recorded and set aside. It extends
  Nelson's computational grounded theory framework with an accountability
  phase: the topic model discovered from civil society's own words is frozen
  and used as a fixed instrument to score the institution's own documents, so
  that all collections are measured on one comparable scale. Piloted on the
  UN's first Global Dialogue on AI Governance, Geneva, July 2026.
keywords:
  - AI governance
  - civil society participation
  - multilateral governance
  - computational grounded theory
  - structural topic model
  - accountability
